      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Definite Integrals",
      "problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\frac{{u}^{2}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
